From 3499d6645a917d554a621d81a7f753aa08355788 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plsy1 <39407459+plsy1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 20:03:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] P5a spike: kernel-maintained per-thread view read by the
 agent (BPFTIME_VIEW=1)

tools/view: a privileged loader (tern_viewd start/status/stop, pinned links)
attaches five hooks (task_newtask, fexit commit_creds with fentry/kprobe
fallbacks, task_rename, sched_process_exec, sched_process_exit) that keep
{tid, tgid, uid, gid, comm} per thread in a BPF_F_MMAPABLE array indexed by
tid & mask, under a seqlock. view_selftest (S0-S10, M0, --bench) and
hookbench test it without Tern.

Runtime: with BPFTIME_VIEW=1 (read at load) the agent maps the view read only
(RW fd, PROT_READ mapping: on 6.12 a shared mapping of a read-only bpf fd is
refused) and bpf_get_current_uid_gid / bpf_get_current_comm read the calling
thread's slot first; a miss (other tid/tgid, odd seq, stale, empty) queries
the kernel. pid/tid still come from the (careful) identity cache, which is
the view key. Off, each helper pays one load and branch.

Known: under same-slot write contention a writer that cannot take the lock
marks the slot stale (reads fall back until the next full refresh); no wrong
value in any selftest.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
